    {
      "calls": [
        {
          "ID": "toolu_01XDhnoKFwrzMHsvQgkohXzK",
          "Name": "submit_read",
          "Input": {
            "assessments": [
              {
                "facts": [
                  "F74",
                  "F32",
                  "F59",
                  "S16:F71",
                  "S16:F72",
                  "S2:F33",
                  "S2:F39",
                  "S3:F38",
                  "S9:F29",
                  "S16:F86"
                ],
                "ref": "N1",
                "statement": "The Stories that moved this week mostly come from a few statements made by a new Prime Minister, Andy Burnham, early in his time in office, and they record declared intentions far more often than completed acts.",
                "supersedes": ""
              },
              {
                "facts": [
                  "S1:F12",
                  "S1:F13",
                  "S2:F15",
                  "S2:F34",
                  "S2:F40",
                  "S2:F48",
                  "S3:F16",
                  "S3:F38",
                  "S2:F33"
                ],
                "ref": "N2",
                "statement": "The UK is stepping up air defence for Ukraine and sanctions on Russia's energy exports instead of waiting on the US-announced reciprocal energy ceasefire, which had no confirmed start date.",
                "supersedes": ""
              },
              {
                "facts": [
                  "S16:F72",
                  "S16:F73",
                  "S16:F86",
                  "S16:F93",
                  "S17:F82",
                  "S16:F83"
                ],
                "ref": "N3",
                "statement": "The only concrete sign so far that Holyrood is engaging with the Prime Minister's offer is the Scottish Government's matched £10 million for defence technical colleges, so the National Economic Council meeting with the First Ministers, due in a couple of weeks from 16 October, is the first real test of the offer.",
                "supersedes": ""
              },
              {
                "facts": [
                  "S10:F50",
                  "S10:F55",
                  "S14:F56"
                ],
                "ref": "N4",
                "statement": "Derby is becoming the physical centre of the rail reorganisation, home both to the Great British Railways headquarters and to the £1 billion fleet of new northern trains being built at Alstom's site there.",
                "supersedes": ""
              }
            ],
            "statements": [
              {
                "cites": [
                  "S1:F12",
                  "S10:F50",
                  "S16:F93",
                  "N1"
                ],
                "text": "Every Development that reached the Record this week is dated 11 to 16 October. Read together, they look less like separate stories than the opening agenda of a new Prime Minister."
              },
              {
                "cites": [
                  "F74",
                  "F59",
                  "F32",
                  "N1"
                ],
                "text": "Andy Burnham told the First Minister during his first week in office that he wanted to work with him. In a few days he then spoke on Ukraine, on Scotland and on the railways."
              },
              {
                "cites": [
                  "S16:F86",
                  "S2:F39",
                  "S3:F38",
                  "S9:F29",
                  "N1"
                ],
                "text": "Most of what was announced is still something the government intends to do, not something it has done: devolving DWP employment support, speeding up missile deliveries, fully implementing the ban on maritime services for Russian LNG, and making AI the centrepiece of the 2027 G20 presidency. Each one is a promise to check against delivery later."
              },
              {
                "cites": [
                  "S1:F12",
                  "S1:F13",
                  "N2"
                ],
                "text": "On Ukraine, the energy ceasefire is the story to watch. President Trump announced a reciprocal one, but Ukraine would say only that it will implement it as soon as a start date is confirmed, so the date was still open."
              },
              {
                "cites": [
                  "S2:F15",
                  "S2:F34",
                  "S2:F40",
                  "S3:F16",
                  "S3:F38",
                  "N2"
                ],
                "text": "London has not waited for that date. It is funding Ukraine through a fifth winter under bombardment, delivering £45 million of surface-to-air missiles and speeding up £10 million of them, and pressing on Russia's energy revenue with sanctions covering over 90% of its oil-producing capacity, plus the LNG ban."
              },
              {
                "cites": [
                  "S2:F33",
                  "S2:F48",
                  "N2"
                ],
                "text": "The request to Ministers for further sanctions, military aid and fiscal packages, along with diplomatic efforts to get other countries supplying defensive equipment, suggests the UK expects to keep up the pressure, not ease off."
              },
              {
                "cites": [
                  "S16:F72",
                  "S16:F73",
                  "S16:F93",
                  "S17:F82",
                  "N3"
                ],
                "text": "In Scotland, the Prime Minister made an offer to the Scottish Government, to be worked through No10 North and the Scotland Office. So far the one concrete reply is Holyrood agreeing to match £10 million for two defence technical excellence colleges."
              },
              {
                "cites": [
                  "S16:F83",
                  "N3"
                ],
                "text": "The National Economic Council's meeting with the First Ministers of Scotland, Wales and Northern Ireland, scheduled for a couple of weeks after 16 October, is the first point at which to judge whether the offer turns into real cooperation."
              },
              {
                "cites": [
                  "S10:F50",
                  "S10:F55",
                  "S14:F56",
                  "N4"
                ],
                "text": "On rail, Derby stands out: it will host the Great British Railways headquarters, with up to 450 staff, and Alstom's Derby site will build the £1 billion fleet of new trains for the north."
              }
            ],
            "stories": []
          }
        }
      ],
